# DevSecOps sign-off on qe-harness/'s dependency-tree license surface
# (see .bmad/handoff-log.md, 2026-08-24, devsecops-engineer row).
# Trivy `license` findings allowlisted below by license ID, not by CVE.
# CAUTION -- this is a repo-wide allowlist, not scoped to reference-sut/pom.xml
# specifically: Trivy's plain-text ignorefile format has no path dimension, and
# an attempted `.trivyignore.yaml` with per-license `paths:` scoping did not
# actually suppress the finding when tested live against this checkout with
# the installed Trivy version (0.72.0 locally, 0.74.0 in CI) -- confirmed by
# injecting a throwaway logback dependency into harness/common/pom.xml (which
# carries no logging dependency today) and finding it was NOT waved through.
# The residual risk this leaves: a future, DIFFERENT dependency anywhere under
# qe-harness/ that happens to carry LGPL-2.1-only or GPL-2.0-with-classpath-
# exception -- not just the two libraries named below -- would also pass this
# gate silently. Bounded by how few real dependencies carry exactly these two
# classifications, and by devsecops-engineer review of any new dependency
# addition, but a real limitation, not a precisely-scoped allowlist. Loaded via
# harness:scan's `--ignorefile qe-harness/.trivyignore` flag in
# .gitlab-ci.yml (Trivy 0.74.0's `fs` command resolves the default
# ".trivyignore" relative to the invocation's working directory, not the
# scanned target directory -- confirmed empirically against this checkout;
# the flag is required for a file living under qe-harness/ to be honored
# when the job invokes `trivy fs ... qe-harness/` from the repo root).
#
# ch.qos.logback:logback-classic, ch.qos.logback:logback-core
# LGPL-2.1-only is Spring Boot's own default logging backend -- every
# Spring Boot application carries this dependency transitively via
# spring-boot-starter. LGPL's dynamic-linking permission is the standard,
# accepted basis for depending on an LGPL library from a differently
# licensed (here proprietary-internal) codebase without inheriting its
# copyleft obligations.
LGPL-2.1-only

# jakarta.annotation:jakarta.annotation-api
# GPL-2.0-with-classpath-exception is specifically designed by its own
# license text to permit linking without imposing GPL obligations on the
# consumer -- the canonical, industry-standard reason every Jakarta/
# GlassFish-licensed API is treated as safe to depend on from a
# proprietary or differently-licensed codebase.
GPL-2.0-with-classpath-exception
